In the Node service, recurring work meant a separate worker process. In .NET it usually lives **inside the web application** as a hosted service — the same process, started by the host and stopped when it shuts down.

```csharp
public class ReportSweeper : BackgroundService
{
    private readonly IServiceScopeFactory _scopes;
    private readonly ILogger<ReportSweeper> _logger;

    public ReportSweeper(IServiceScopeFactory scopes, ILogger<ReportSweeper> logger)
    {
        _scopes = scopes;
        _logger = logger;
    }

    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                using var scope = _scopes.CreateScope();
                var jobs = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<IReportJobs>();
                await jobs.RunDueAsync(stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                break;                                   // shutting down: expected
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                _logger.LogError(ex, "Report sweep failed; continuing");
            }

            await Task.Delay(TimeSpan.FromMinutes(1), stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}
```

Four things in there are the lesson.

**The scope.** A `BackgroundService` is a singleton, and a `DbContext` is scoped — per request, and there are no requests here. Injecting the context directly into the constructor is the **captive dependency** problem: a scoped object captured by a singleton lives for the life of the process, accumulating tracked entities and eventually failing in ways that are very hard to read. Create a scope per iteration and resolve from it.

**The cancellation token, threaded all the way down.** `stoppingToken` is passed to `RunDueAsync` and to `Task.Delay`. Without that, shutdown waits for a sleeping loop, the host gives up after its timeout, and the process is killed mid-work.

**Catching, and continuing.** An unhandled exception in `ExecuteAsync` ends the service. On modern .NET it can also stop the host — but the older, worse behaviour is what makes this worth stating: a background service that dies quietly while the web application keeps serving traffic is a genuinely nasty failure, because nothing looks broken. Catch, log, keep looping.

**Distinguishing the expected cancellation.** `OperationCanceledException` during shutdown is normal and should not be logged as an error, or every clean restart produces a false alarm.

Now the part that connects to V2. If a deployment can stop the process at any moment — and it can, since deploying is exactly that — then **every job must be safe to run again**. Interrupted work is a normal event, not an exception, and that makes idempotency a requirement rather than a nicety. Persist job state, make effects idempotent (an upsert keyed on the job's identity rather than a blind insert), and record completion in the same transaction as the effect. If those two are separate commits, an interruption between them means the work is done and unrecorded, and the rerun does it twice.

And deployment ordering is what you already know from V1 chapter 12, with one .NET-specific note. Additive migrations first, then the new code, then destructive migrations once nothing refers to the old shape. The .NET note: `dotnet ef database update` is convenient in development and a poor fit for production, where several instances may start simultaneously and race to apply the same migration. Generate a script (`dotnet ef migrations script --idempotent`) and apply it as a distinct, single deployment step instead.

items.workedExampleMarkdown:
A nightly report job, and what a deployment does to it.

**The unsafe version:**

```csharp
public async Task RunDueAsync(CancellationToken ct)
{
    var due = await _db.ReportJobs
        .Where(j => j.Status == "pending" && j.DueAt <= _clock.UtcNow)
        .ToListAsync(ct);

    foreach (var job in due)
    {
        var report = await _builder.BuildAsync(job, ct);   // expensive
        _db.Reports.Add(report);
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(ct);                     // commit 1: the effect

        job.Status = "done";
        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(ct);                     // commit 2: the record
    }
}
```

Deploy between the two saves and the report exists while the job still says `pending`. The next instance picks it up and builds a **second** report. Nobody sees an error; the customer sees duplicate reports, which is worse than a failure because it is not obviously wrong.

**The safe version:**

```csharp
public async Task RunDueAsync(CancellationToken ct)
{
    var due = await _db.ReportJobs
        .Where(j => j.Status == "pending" && j.DueAt <= _clock.UtcNow)
        .ToListAsync(ct);

    foreach (var job in due)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();

        var report = await _builder.BuildAsync(job, ct);
        report.JobId = job.Id;                 // unique index on Reports.JobId

        _db.Reports.Add(report);
        job.Status = "done";
        job.CompletedAt = _clock.UtcNow;

        await _db.SaveChangesAsync(ct);        // one transaction: effect AND record
    }
}
```

Two defences, deliberately overlapping. The single save means the report and the completion commit together, so an interruption leaves the job `pending` with no report — the correct state to retry from. And the unique index on `Reports.JobId` means that even if something contrives to run the job twice, the second insert is rejected by the database rather than producing a duplicate. The transaction is the design; the constraint is what catches the case the design did not anticipate.

Note also `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between jobs: shutdown stops the loop at a job boundary rather than halfway through building a report.

